#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167691} :activity/id #uuid "adc0eea0-4c11-5878-9c7e-48c4aba23d56" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168082 :tutorial/content "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is **conservative** if there exists a scalar function $f$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\! f.\n$$\n\nIf such a function $f$ exists, it is called a **potential function** for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNot all vector fields are conservative. However, conservative vector fields have many interesting and useful properties, as we'll soon discover." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1462096-7305-5fe4-ae99-253541521cba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5152 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1992388a-9592-55ad-8541-266b5f3c9669" :step/math-academy-id 32405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382703}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"} :step/id #uuid "774cb7e3-782d-56db-8b29-9c8786c0396b" :step/math-academy-id 32593 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "218a6bf7-b94b-5fa6-bb10-cfd37d79dc0e" :step/math-academy-id 35579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166717 :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383055}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "2a21469f-f98e-54de-806c-b0901c562c75" :step/math-academy-id 35582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168124 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that this is equivalent to saying that the scalar curl is zero everywhere on $D,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0.$$\n\nIf $D\\neq\\mathbb R^2,$ then the situation gets a little trickier. We'll consider this case in a separate lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a5b26c9-caa5-579e-a57c-c42b07eebafe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5806 :tutorial/title "A Test to Determine Whether a Given Vector Field is Conservative"} :step/id #uuid "d7a42f9a-6688-59d2-b357-eb5701925961" :step/math-academy-id 35586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167748}}] :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166975}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410579} :activity/id #uuid "5445d5ff-8221-5ab1-9733-fcfbdf6c3d6a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd4d74c-d28c-5067-b253-93fff9b615be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410564} {:answer/id #uuid "96aeda3b-433c-5c5a-a89e-38dda14fc2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410567} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d7b4b6-5f16-50df-84c9-3952694f88d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410568} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb3baff-2a23-5160-bf8a-97ba1ff3048b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410569} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0937dc-40e4-5230-9016-9a4210085b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410564} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4eb193-01d2-584e-8123-beed2a93cb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a79f93a0-28bb-5df3-afcd-772f79c2b956"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f11833-470e-5bef-bed8-6e5dd7f35fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238821} {:answer/id #uuid "73bfaf49-d235-5587-b751-402d142ac763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239046} {:answer/id #uuid "a836c130-129a-569d-9e28-b1933339a9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239516} {:answer/id #uuid "b09885ad-d9c7-552d-b4c7-930d5c59c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239589} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3240e0-b339-53b6-a701-9e9f4809ca9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239589} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa88a49-b814-5ee5-80f8-03e880fa2400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" :question/math-academy-id "q-104573" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "b4f0c749-c70e-5cd0-8997-a35907ffaa6a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410563} {:answer/id #uuid "78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410565} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23f760-5ac7-5ae1-99ac-01f57b186e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "ll only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410566} {:answer/id #uuid "eef188a5-b79b-535e-a698-3abfd5aa7c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410571} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9201b9-0d51-54ab-bcd2-eefc57d9346d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "l only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410565} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "e11a097e-2d0f-542d-a29a-07a9eed3fead" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410578}}] :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410562}]}
